1. Kubernetes basic commands
kubectl is the command-line tool used to communicate with a Kubernetes cluster.
Command	                                                Description
kubectl version	                                  Displays Kubernetes version information.
kubectl cluster-info	                            Displays cluster information.
kubectl get nodes	                                Lists all cluster nodes.
kubectl describe node <name>	                    Displays details of a node.
kubectl get namespaces	                          Lists all namespaces.
kubectl get all	                                  Lists common resources in the current namespace.
kubectl get all -A	                              Lists common resources across namespaces.


2. Pod commands
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It contains one or more containers.
Command	                                                 Description
kubectl get pods	                                 Lists Pods.
kubectl get pods -o wide	                         Displays Pods with additional details.
kubectl get pods -A	                               Lists Pods in all namespaces.
kubectl describe pod nginx	                       Displays details of the NGINX Pod.
kubectl logs nginx	                               Displays Pod logs.
kubectl logs -f nginx	                             Continuously displays Pod logs.
kubectl exec -it nginx -- sh	                     Opens a shell inside the Pod.
kubectl delete pod nginx	                         Deletes the Pod.
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx	                   Creates an NGINX Pod.
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx --port=80	         Creates an NGINX Pod with port 80 specified.


3. ReplicaSet commands
A ReplicaSet maintains the required number of Pod replicas.
Command	                                                  Description
kubectl get replicasets	                            Lists ReplicaSets.
kubectl get rs	                                    Short form to list ReplicaSets.
kubectl describe rs <name>	                        Displays ReplicaSet details.
kubectl scale rs <name> --replicas=5	              Sets ReplicaSet replicas to 5.
kubectl delete rs <name>	                          Deletes a ReplicaSet.


4. Deployment commands
A Deployment manages Pods and ReplicaSets. It supports scaling and rolling updates.
Command	                                                                      Description
kubectl get deployments	                                                Lists Deployments.
kubectl get deploy	                                                    Short form to list Deployments.
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx	                          Creates an NGINX Deployment.
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=5	            Creates a Deployment with 5 replicas.
kubectl describe deployment nginx	                                      Displays Deployment details.
kubectl scale deployment nginx --replicas=5	                            Scales Deployment to 5 replicas.
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx	                                Checks rollout status.
kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx	                              Displays rollout history.
kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx	                                  Reverts the latest rollout.
kubectl set image deployment/nginx nginx=nginx:1.27	                    Updates the container image.
kubectl delete deployment nginx	                                        Deletes the Deployment.


5. Service commands
A Service provides a stable network endpoint to access Pods.
Command	                                                                  Description
kubectl get services	                                                  Lists Services.
kubectl get svc	                                                        Short form to list Services.
kubectl describe svc nginx	                                            Displays Service details.
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --type=ClusterIP	            Exposes Deployment internally.
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --type=NodePort	              Exposes Deployment through a node port.
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --type=LoadBalancer	          Exposes Deployment using a load balancer.
kubectl delete svc nginx	                                              Deletes a Service.

Service types
Type	                    Description
ClusterIP	             Accessible inside the cluster.
NodePort	             Accessible through a port on the node.
LoadBalancer	         Exposes the Service through a load balancer.


6. Kubernetes YAML commands
YAML files define Kubernetes resources such as Pods, Deployments and Services.
Command	                                                Description
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml	                          Creates or updates resources from YAML.
kubectl create -f pod.yaml	                        Creates resources from YAML.
kubectl get -f pod.yaml	                            Displays the resource specified in YAML.
kubectl delete -f pod.yaml	                        Deletes resources specified in YAML.
kubectl diff -f pod.yaml	                          Shows configuration differences.
kubectl get pods -o yaml	                          Displays Pods in YAML format.
kubectl get deployment nginx -o yaml	              Displays Deployment configuration in YAML.
kubectl explain pod	                                Explains Pod fields.
kubectl explain deployment.spec	                    Explains Deployment specification fields.


7. Lab: Run an NGINX Pod
This is explicitly mentioned in your syllabus.
Method 1: Using command
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod nginx
kubectl logs nginx


Description:
- kubectl run creates the Pod.
- kubectl get pods checks its status.
- kubectl describe displays details.
- kubectl logs displays application logs.
Method 2: Using YAML
Create nginx-pod.yaml:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx
spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80


Apply the YAML:
kubectl apply -f nginx-pod.yaml
kubectl get pods


Description: kubectl apply -f creates the Pod using the YAML configuration.




8. Lab: NGINX Deployment with 5 replicas
This is also explicitly mentioned in your syllabus.
Method 1: Using commands
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx
kubectl scale deployment nginx --replicas=5
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods


Description:
- Creates an NGINX Deployment.
- Scales it to 5 replicas.
- Checks the Deployment and Pods.
Method 2: Using YAML
Create nginx-deployment.yaml:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx
spec:
  replicas: 5
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80


Apply:
kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get replicasets
kubectl get pods


Description: The YAML creates a Deployment that maintains 5 NGINX Pods.



9. Lab: Access NGINX using a Service
Your syllabus requires accessing the Deployment using a Service.

Create nginx-service.yaml:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: nginx-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: nginx
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080


Apply:
kubectl apply -f nginx-service.yaml
kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc nginx-service


Description:
- kubectl apply creates the Service.
- kubectl get svc displays the Service.
- kubectl describe svc displays its configuration.
Access the application through a local cluster using port forwarding:
kubectl port-forward service/nginx-service 8080:80


Open:
http://localhost:8080


This forwards local port 8080 to port 80 of the Service.


